# Frankie and Johnny: traditional American ballad (St. Louis, 1899). Words as printed in
# Sandburg, The American Songbag (1927), "Frankie and Johnny", stanzas 1, 3, 4 and 7.
{title: Frankie and Johnny}
{composer: Traditional (USA)}
{key: G}
{tempo: 100}
{time: 4/4}
{x_level: 2}
{x_strum: D - D U D - D U}

{start_of_grid label="Verse"}
| G . . . | G . . . |
| G . . . | G . . . |
| C . . . | C . . . |
| G . . . | G . . . |
| D7 . . . | C . . . | G . . . | G . D7 . |
{end_of_grid}

{comment: 12-bar blues: G for four bars, C for two, G for two, then D7 C G G. Swing the eighths (long-short).}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
[G]Frankie and Johnny were lovers,
O lordy how they could love.
[C]Swore to be true to each other,
[G]True as the stars above;
He was her [D7]man but he [C]done her [G]wrong, so wrong.[D7]
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
[G]Frankie and Johnny went walking,
Johnny in his bran' new suit,
[C]"O good Lawd," says Frankie,
"Don't my [G]Johnny look cute?"
He was her [D7]man but he [C]done her [G]wrong, so wrong.[D7]
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 3"}
[G]Frankie went down to the corner,
To buy a glass of beer;
She [C]says to the fat bartender,
"Has my [G]lovinest man been here?
He was my [D7]man but he's [C]done me [G]wrong, so wrong."[D7]
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 4"}
[G]Frankie went down to the hotel,
Looked in the window so high,
There she [C]saw her lovin' Johnny
A-[G]lovin' up Alice Bly;
He was her [D7]man but he [C]done her [G]wrong, so wrong.[D7]
{end_of_verse}

{comment: Four of the ballad's many verses, as Sandburg printed them in 1927.}
{x_end: G}
